// Workspace so one gate command can cover every module in the tree.
//
// tools/decode-oracle is a separate module for LICENSING reasons: it embeds
// QEMU's decodetree tables (LGPL-2.1-or-later) and is licensed the same way,
// while raptormark itself is Apache-2.0. Its nested go.mod is what keeps that
// obligation out of the pipeline -- and it is also what made its tests
// invisible to a repo-root `go test ./...`.
//
// ⚠️ A workspace does NOT make `./...` recursive across modules. Measured, not
// assumed: with this file in place, `go list ./...` from the repo root still
// returns zero decode-oracle packages, because a relative pattern is resolved
// against the module you are standing in. What the workspace buys is that the
// other module's packages are RESOLVABLE by module path from here, so the gate
// names both:
//
//	go build ./... raptormark/tools/decode-oracle/...
//	go vet   ./... raptormark/tools/decode-oracle/...
//	go test  ./... raptormark/tools/decode-oracle/...
//
// ❌ Not `go test all`. In workspace mode `all` also pulls in dependencies --
// 152 packages here, including kong -- so it would run third-party tests and
// fail for reasons that are not ours.
//
// ⚠️ This changes which packages those commands VISIT. It does not change what
// anything links. cmd/raptormark does not import the decode oracle, no
// Apache-2.0 binary gains an LGPL dependency, and the two modules stay
// separately licensed and separately distributable. Compiling a package during
// a gate is not distributing a combined work.
//
// If you add a module, add it here: internal/builder's
// TestWorkspaceCoversEveryModule fails otherwise, which is the point -- a
// module missing from this file is silently ungated.
//
// GOWORK=off disables it, if you ever need to build a module in isolation.

go 1.26.0

use (
	.
	./tools/decode-oracle
)
